{"author":{"id":"127a58c7f014820991be95805df42525db12c92e2d7319d04d4df105a9aa3444"},"ops":[{"type":3,"timestamp":1788297608,"nonce":"G0VIWJyhXkiPnAyxkTSXbUzhWfU=","message":"THE WHITELIST'S OBJECTION WAS TO A SECOND SOURCE OF TRUTH, NOT TO OBSERVATION.\n`VerbArgs` now keeps a READ LOG; `supplied − queried` is the unknown-argument\nset. Branch `spec/arg-names`, commits `67f45db`, `5d2fd50`, `2d22a78`. Builds\nclean (`dotnet build -c Release`, 0 warnings 0 errors); the branch carries no\nassembly. Session 21, worker D.\n\n**NOTHING HAS MET A BENCH.** Not one verb has been dispatched. The pure logic\nis unit-checked offline (32/32, a scratch console project over an extracted\ncopy of `VerbArgs`) and `accept/s13-mod-surface.py --selftest` is 30/30, was\n25/25 — which the runner itself describes as \"the ASSERTIONS work; nothing\nabout the mod was asserted\".\n\n## The mechanism, and why it is not the declaration session 15 rejected\n\nSession 15's measurement stands and is not re-litigated. 120 verbs; 22 handlers\nthat read nothing at their own call site; 88 `(verb, key)` pairs the suites send\nthrough shared helpers; five suite call sites that build their dict at runtime.\n\nEvery accessor on `VerbArgs` — `Has`, `Raw`, `Str`, `StrReq`, `Bool`, `Num`,\n`NumReq`, `Int`, `IntReq`, `Long`, `StrList`, `NearMiss` — now funnels through\none private `Look(key)` that marks the key. Zero declarations, all 120 verbs,\nand nothing to update when a verb gains an argument (which matters this round,\nwith worker B's `advance {unread_ok, through_casualties}` and worker C's\n`posture` landing beside it).\n\n**The 22-forwarder case is not a special case**: the log follows the OBJECT, and\n`TimeDriver.Start(ctx.Command, ctx.Args)` hands over the same `VerbArgs`.\nRuntime-built caller dicts are a non-issue because nothing is compared against a\nlist. `trade-set` is the proof by construction — its singular sugar reads\n`foreach (var k in new[]{\"thing\",\"index\",\"count\",\"buy\",\"sell\"}) if\n(ctx.Args.Has(k))`, a key no static scan can see and the read log gets for free.\n\n**Forwarding audit.** Nine `new VerbArgs(...)`: one dispatch root; seven NESTED\nSUB-OBJECT parses over a value the parent already read via `Raw(key)`\n(`until.event`, `until.condition`, `filter`, `set`, `drugs[i]`, `set[i]`,\n`elements[i]`) which must NOT union into the parent or an inner key would count\nas a top-level read; and `StarterKit.Sub`, the only real forward, which needs no\nlink either because it hands the child a dict the PARENT CONSTRUCTED out of\nvalues it had to read. A nested object's own unknown keys stay unchecked — the\nacknowledged gap.\n\n## THE FORM SHIPPED: REPORT, plus REFUSE where a default mutates\n\nI measured the cost of refusing and it is too high. 729 accessor call sites;\n~290 conditional; **73 keys across 26 verbs are read only on SOME paths while\nthe verb still returns success.** Four named shapes, all legitimate:\n\n1. **`dry_run` skips the block that reads the keys.** `ZoneVerbs.Add` calls\n   `Shape()` — the only reader of `label`, `priority`, `filter`, `plant`,\n   `allow_sow`, `allow_cut` — under `if (!dryRun)`. So `zone {op:\"add\", plant,\n   label, dry_run:true}`, the exact preflight an agent should run, would be\n   refused.\n2. **A fallback unread on the happy path.** `dev:spawn-thing` reads `pos` only\n   when `stockpile` is absent or storage refused — the documented \"store it,\n   else drop it here\" call.\n3. **`queue` after the per-pawn gates, in twelve verbs** (`attack`, `equip`,\n   `wear`, `drop`, `consume`, `extinguish`, `beat-fire`, `tend`, `repair`,\n   `man-turret`, `rest-until-healed`, `TakeToBed`). `wear {pawn, thing,\n   queue:true}` refused by its gate is a SUCCESS envelope with `queue` unread —\n   and `accept/4087644-order-honesty.py` is a suite about exactly those refusals.\n4. **Whole-verb refusals return before the config block.** `bill-add` answers\n   `NotAWorkTable` before `ValidateBillArgs` reads its twenty levers, so\n   refusing would replace an informative refusal with \"unknown args\". Same for\n   `bill-set`, `bill-remove`, `trade-start`/`trade-set`/`trade-confirm`,\n   `comms-call`, `quest-accept`, `surgery-remove`, `construction`.\n\n`rwa/selftest.sh`'s `ping --n 500 --flag --word Normal` — a deliberate CLI-cast\nexercise against a verb that reads only `echo` — would have started failing too.\n\nSo `Result` gains `IgnoredArgs` and the poller writes a TOP-LEVEL\n`ignored_args {keys, read, detail, journal_seq_from?, journal_seq_to?}`, present\nONLY when a key went unread, so no correct command's envelope changes shape. And\n`Log.Warning` — already patched by `JournalHooks` — puts the same finding in the\njournal as a durable `warning` row, so a ten-day run can be audited for dropped\narguments afterwards. That half is main-thread only (a `MainThread=false`\nhandler may not touch Verse). `advance` returns `DeferredResult` and\n`TimeDriver` writes its own result, so the journal row is its only channel.\n\nThe seq range is there because the report is post-dispatch by construction: when\nthe verb mutated, \"what did that dropped argument cost me\" must be answerable\nfrom the envelope. The suggestion in `detail` is Levenshtein against the keys\nTHIS CALL READ — derived, so it works for arguments added later — while\n`NearMiss`'s call-site alias list still carries `at` -\u003e `pos`, an edit distance\nof 3 no conservative rule would find.\n\n## The destructive shape, swept and closed\n\nComment #7 named the shape, not just the instance: \"a defaulted list argument\nwhose default is non-empty and whose steps mutate\". The tree has exactly three.\n\n| verb | default `steps` | what it does |\n|---|---|---|\n| `journal-selftest` | letter, message, error, **downed**, **break** | `HealthUtility.DamageUntilDowned`, forced Berserk |\n| `pawn-fixture` | **wound**, **sadden**, **tatter** | damages a pawn, stacks thoughts, destroys apparel |\n| `world-fixture` | bench, bill, stockpiles, growing, research, letter | spawns, bills, zones, completes research, letters |\n\nAll three now call `VerbArgs.RefuseStray(op, ownArgs, …)` BEFORE the first step\n— unconditionally, not only when the default fires, since every step in all\nthree mutates. `ownArgs` is MESSAGE-ONLY: the detection is the read log, which\nconsults it not at all, so its drift mode is a worse sentence and never a\nrefused legitimate call. That inverted asymmetry is the whole objection to the\nrejected 120-verb form. It is also what makes refusing safe here despite the\nconditional-read finding above — `journal-selftest`'s step-gated `save_name`,\n`power_lamps` and `error_text` are in the list, so they are accepted whether or\nnot their step ran.\n\n## Blast radius, measured statically and completely\n\n294 distinct `(op, key)` pairs across 107 ops, from every `accept/*.py|ps1|sh|md`,\n`rwa`, `rwa/selftest.sh`, `playbook/`, `templates/` and `checklists/`. Every one\ncross-checked against the keys the mod can read.\n\n**Keys sent by a shipped caller that no verb ever reads: three, all in one\ncall.** `rwa/selftest.sh:156` sends `ping --n 500 --flag --word Normal --pos\n120,130`, which is a test of the CLI's type casting against a verb that reads\nonly `echo`. It asserts on the COMMAND file and expects exit 0 — preserved\nunder report-only, broken under refusal. No suite line was changed.\n\nFour apparent hits were false alarms of the static scan, resolved by reading:\n`quest`/`quest-accept`/`quest-dismiss {quest}` (read via `QuestArg(ctx.Args,\n\"quest\")`), `warden {enable}` (`ModeList(a, \"enable\")`), `trade-set {buy}` (the\nloop-variable read above), and `journal-selftest {fade_seconds}`\n(`accept/1.8-game-clock-advance.sh:437`, inside a heredoc of prose describing a\nstep that does not exist — not a send). `construction {layout}` is already\nrefused on purpose by an existing `NearMiss`, and `accept/fc287ba:450` asserts\nthat refusal.\n\n## The mechanism guards itself, statically\n\n`accept/s13-mod-surface.py` phase 9 re-derives from the source the way 9.7\nre-derives the ladder table:\n\n- **9.9a** matches every `raw.` touch inside `VerbArgs` WITH ITS ARGUMENTS and\n  expects exactly two — `TryGetValue` inside `Look()`, `ContainsKey` probing a\n  `NearMiss` alias (which must not be marked, or a stray key would be masked by\n  the check that names it). An accessor added later that reads the backing\n  dictionary directly would make a legitimate key look unread: the rejected\n  declaration's failure mode sneaking back in through the mechanism that\n  replaced it.\n- **9.10a-c** re-derive the three `RefuseStray` lists and fail on drift.\n\nBoth were proved to FAIL before being kept.\n\n## Acceptance\n\n- **1.10a-o** (new) — `digest {wibble:3}` succeeds and publishes\n  `ignored_args`; a clean call publishes none; the report lists the keys the\n  verb read; a one-edit typo is suggested from those keys; and 1.10l-o is\n  comment #6's exact bench call, `dev:spawn-thing --def WoodLog --pos … --mode\n  direct --wibble 3`, now carrying the journal seq range it wrote.\n- **1.11a-h** (new) — comment #7's suggested bullet verbatim.\n  `journal-selftest {kind:\"save\"}` refused with `bad-args`, naming `kind`,\n  naming the arguments it does accept, saying nothing was mutated, publishing\n  no `data` — and **1.11g**, the check that proves it: no `dev`, `downed` or\n  `mental_break` row written between the call and its refusal. 1.11h is the\n  control (`{steps:[\"message\"]}` still runs).\n- **1.8a-e / 1.9a-d** unchanged — `at` -\u003e `pos` and `pos_source` still ship.\n\n## BULLET 1 AS LITERALLY WRITTEN IS STILL NOT MET\n\nSaying it in those words, because comment #5's honesty about it is why this\nissue stayed useful.\n\n\"A verb call carrying an unrecognised argument key is REFUSED with `bad-args`\nnaming the key\" is now true for three verbs (`journal-selftest`,\n`pawn-fixture`, `world-fixture`) and for the `NearMiss` aliases. Everywhere\nelse the call SUCCEEDS and publishes `ignored_args`. I measured the cost of the\nliteral form and it refuses legitimate calls in 26 verbs, including four shapes\nthat shipped suites exercise on purpose.\n\nWhat IS met, for all 120 verbs and with no declaration anywhere:\n\n- **The defect in the title is fixed.** An unknown argument name is no longer\n  silent. Every dropped key is named in the envelope and in the journal.\n- **The destructive instance is refused before it can act**, which is the\n  bullet comment #7 asked for and the reason this issue was promoted.\n- **The inconsistency comment #7 drew out is gone**: a typo'd VALUE and a\n  typo'd KEY both produce a message now, rather than a well-mannered refusal\n  and silent damage.\n\nWhat is left is the scope decision comment #6 already framed, now with numbers\nbehind it: accept the report form for the general case and reword bullet 1, or\nkeep the literal property as an open goal knowing it costs 73 keys across 26\nverbs. Left open rather than closed against a bullet its own text does not\nsatisfy.","files":null}]}